#---
# purpose: an extern's declared return is its own FunctionContract applied to each activation's row, not a shared un-instantiated declaration
#---
#
# `send/2` forwards to `fz_send(pid, t) :: t when t: any`, a prelude extern
# with no fz body of its own. An ordinary function's return picks up its
# argument's type because the BODY carries it there; an extern has no body,
# so the same relation comes from applying the extern's own contract to each
# activation's row -- the one call-instantiation calculator every specced
# function already uses (`FunctionContract::apply`, `Types::match_arrow`).
#
# `ascending/0` and `descending/0` are the same two calls in each order. Each
# call keys its own activation of `send/2` -- `inputs=[pid, int]` and
# `inputs=[pid, float]` -- and each activation defines its own return from its
# own row instead of both sharing whichever type happened to be interned
# first, which is what made the un-applied declaration order-dependent.
#
# Paper answer.
#
#   ascending/0   send(me, 11); send(me, 10.5)   => float
#   descending/0  send(me, 10.5); send(me, 11)   => int
#
# A function's return is the type of its last expression, so each of these is
# the return of its own final `send` call: `send(me, 10.5)` for `ascending/0`
# and `send(me, 11)` for `descending/0`. The two differ only because each call
# site's activation of the extern applies its contract to its own argument.
# Without that application both would read the same declared type and the
# answer would depend on which was interned first.
#
# Execution prints the two returned values, in call order.
def ascending() do
  me = self()
  send(me, 11)
  send(me, 10.5)
end

def descending() do
  me = self()
  send(me, 10.5)
  send(me, 11)
end

def main() do
  dbg(ascending())
  dbg(descending())
end
